+# Android app shell entry points
+
+## Ownership
+
+The Android shell owns how an operating-system launch reaches the shared UI: the manifest intent filters, `MainActivity`'s intent handling, and the app-local plugins registered before the bridge starts. What an `openchamber://` URL means, and which draft or surface it opens, belongs to `packages/ui/src/apps/deepLinks.ts` and `deepLinkNavigation.ts`. iOS launch entry points belong to `packages/mobile/ios`.
+
+## Invariants
+
+- Every launch the app claims (custom scheme, assistant gesture) reaches the shared deep-link handler through the same `openchamber://` vocabulary. The shell adds no second parser and no second URL shape.
+- An intent that the shell does not claim passes through unchanged, so unrelated launches keep the bridge's default behaviour.
+
+## Decisions
+
+### Why the assistant intent is rewritten into a deep link and not delivered through a plugin
+
+`ACTION_ASSIST` carries no data URI, so the Capacitor App plugin ignores it. Rewriting the intent before the bridge starts reuses the existing cold- and warm-launch path, including the retained cold-launch URL, instead of adding a plugin, an event name and a JS listener for one gesture. The rewrite happens in `onCreate` before `super.onCreate` because the bridge reads the launch intent while starting.
